# rp2350_pizero

A Waveshare RP2350-PiZero: an RP2350B, the 48-GPIO package, with 16MB of flash
and an unpopulated PSRAM footprint, on a Raspberry Pi Zero-sized board with a
40-pin header, a DVI connector, a TF card socket, a native USB-C port, a
PIO-USB USB-C port, a lithium battery charger, and BOOT and RUN keys.

It derives from pico2-arm for what pico2-arm says: the RP2350's Arm Secure
cores, the Pico SDK, the ABI, and the rp2350 machine. What pico2-arm cannot
say is the package. pico2.h defines PICO_RP2350A as one, unconditionally, so a
build under it has thirty GPIOs, and the TF card, the DVI pins, and the PSRAM
select on GP30 to GP47 would not exist. The Pico bridge's board table
therefore selects the SDK's own waveshare_rp2350_pizero header for this board
in place of pico2's: PICO_RP2350A zero, 16MB of flash, the default UART as
UART1 on GP4 and GP5, and no LED. The chain, and everything the model derives
from it, is unchanged.

From Waveshare's schematic, RP2350-PiZero.pdf:

- The 40-pin header carries all of GP0 to GP27 in the Raspberry Pi's
  functional places, not its BCM numbers: SDA and SCL, pins 3 and 5, are GP2
  and GP3; TX and RX, pins 8 and 10, are GP4 and GP5 where a Pi has BCM14 and
  15; the SPI's MOSI, MISO, and SCLK, pins 19, 21, and 23, are GP11, GP12, and
  GP10, SPI1, with CE0 and CE1 on GP8 and GP7; ID_SDA and ID_SCL, pins 27 and
  28, are GP0 and GP1. The rest fall where they fall -- GP14 on pin 7, GP15
  on pin 29, GP26 on pin 37, GP27 on pin 13 -- so a Pi HAT's signals reach
  the RP2350 under other numbers than its BCM ones.
- The TF socket is wired for four-bit SDIO -- clock GP30, command GP31, data
  GP40 to GP43 -- which in SPI mode is clock GP30, MOSI GP31, MISO GP40, and
  CS GP43.
- The DVI connector's three data pairs and clock pair are GP32 to GP39, its
  CEC, SCL, and SDA GP46, GP45, and GP44. They are not the HSTX pins, GP12 to
  GP19, so DVI here is PIO's.
- The PIO-USB port's D+ is GP28 and D- GP29, through 22 ohms each.
- GP47 is the PSRAM footprint's chip select.
- The only LED is a red power indicator on 3V3, which no GPIO drives, so the
  board reports no LED. SWD is a three-pin header on the chip's own SWCLK and
  SWDIO.
- ADC_AVDD is the 3V3 rail, so the ADC reference is 3300 mV. The RP2350B's
  ADC inputs are GP40 to GP47, and the TF card and DVI hold every one of them.

The board table claims neither a second I2C nor a second UART: the default
UART is already UART1, and the SDK header's default I2C is I2C1 on the
header's GP2 and GP3, so the instance-one wirings the six vendor boards carry
would collide with both. None of it is qualified on hardware.

https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/RP2350-PiZero
